# hilavitkutin-contracts, Design Overview

This crate is the surface a consumer writes against, kept apart from any runner so that a later runner, fused, threaded or on another target, demands nothing more of an author or an implementor than the one-thread reference runner does. An author writes kernels against a domain; an implementor writes a store and a visiting order; neither sees the other, and neither sees a runner. What joins them is data: a table of bindings saying which stored column fills which column a kernel names, checked once by `bind`.

`#![no_std]`, no alloc, no `dyn`, no `#![feature]`, no `unsafe`. It depends on `notko` for `Maybe` and `Outcome`, which it re-exports so a crate written against it names one dependency, and on `notko-hlist` for the type-level list. It depends on no other hilavitkutin crate, and on no arvo crate yet: the numbers it needs are proxies here, each going at the swap to arvo.

## The value kernel

A kernel is a type implementing `ValueKernel`. It has no receiver and no context: `at` is handed the values of its reads at one record and returns the values of its writes there. It is handed no position, no range and no view, and asks for none; where a record is, which records are visited and in which morsels is the runner's and the store's. Its reads are one list of members of its domain, its writes a list of `Own` members of the same domain. The two lists are type-level lists, and `at` takes and returns their value projection, a nested pair an author destructures and never indexes:

```rust
fn at((here, (east, (rate, ()))): (L, (L, (L, ())))) -> (L, ());
```

A kernel declares its identity, `ID: KernelId`, because a kernel is bound by data and so one kernel type is many units, and an order key needs an identity that is the same across builds. A `KernelId` is below 128 today: two kernels of one list declaring one id fail the build where the list is bound, by a fold of the ids into a 128-bit word. That word is a stand-in for arvo's masks and its width is no interface.

`Kernels` is a list of kernels of one domain, sealed: the walk that finds a binding's kernel by id and the id fold live on a private supertrait.

## Domains, columns, resources and members

A `Domain` is a set of records each with a key, in key order. Its `Key` orders the records, its `Step` is an offset between keys with one component per axis, and its `Shape` is the run-time extent a store holds. The domain says how to walk its keys from a shape (`first`, `next`) and where a step from a key lands (`reach`). Everything a store needs to lay out records and answer a neighbour read comes from these, so a store is written once for every domain.

A `Column` is a value per record of a domain, a `Resource` one value constant over a step. Named in a kernel, a column is a role: which stored column fills it is data, supplied by a binding. A column declares `OUTSIDE`, what a neighbour read outside the shape reads; that is the first boundary policy and it is unlocked.

The members of a read list are `Own<C>`, this record's value of `C`; `Near<C, O>`, the value at the offset `O` from this record's key, where `O` is an `Offset` the domain's author declares with its step as plain data; and `Param<R>`, a resource's value supplied per binding as a coefficient. A write list holds `Own` members only. `Reads` and `Writes` say a list is one of these, and the walk over each list that checks a binding, mints its handles and reads and writes a record is sealed.

A `Value` is what a column or resource holds: a `Copy` type with one of the closed set of cell widths (`Cell8`, `Cell16`, `Cell32`, `Cell64`), a `FormatId` naming its encoding, and a way into the digest. A store keeps cells as `Word`s and a value moves through its cell.

## The bind and the checked table

A `Binding` is one line of the content table: a `UnitId`, the `KernelId` it runs, a `Role` for each of the kernel's column roles (its reads' `Own` and `Near` members, then its writes') naming a stored column and the format the binding says it is in, and a `Coefficient` for each `Param` member. `bind` checks a table of bindings against a kernel list and a store's declared formats and returns either the checked table `Bound` or the first refusal as a `BindRefusal` value: an unknown kernel, a role or coefficient whose format is not the kernel's or the store's, an unknown column, a wrong count of roles or coefficients, two bindings of one unit, or two write roles naming one stored column (`TwoWritersOfOneColumn`). In a step with a committed view and staged columns, two writers of one staged column have no order a priority could resolve, so they are refused rather than ordered.

`Bound` has no public field and no public constructor, so every runner takes a table that passed. The typed column handle `Col<D, V>` is minted from a passing binding and from nothing else: the format comparison is made once, at bind, and a store reads and writes through handles without comparing formats again. `Bound::each` hands every unit, as a `Unit` with its kernel known as a type, to a `UnitFn`, which is how a runner is written over every kernel of a list without naming one.

## The store and the visiting order

A `Store` holds one shape of one domain's records, its stored columns each in a declared format, as a committed view and staged columns. `step` borrows both for one step, `Read` on the committed view and `Write` on the staged columns; `swap` makes the staged columns committed, and a column no unit wrote keeps its values across the swap. A record is found by its position, an `Index` in key order; positions are the store's and the runner's and no kernel is handed one. How a store lays cells out behind a position is its own.

A `Visit` hands a `Visitor` every position below the extent exactly once, in morsels of the asked size but the last.

## Numbers

`num` holds the proxies this crate uses in place of bare primitives: `Bool`, `USize`, and the two distinct position and quantity types `Index` and `Count`, whose host doors `from_host` and `host` are the only bare `usize` at a public position and which have no `raw`. A `Count` cannot stand where an `Index` goes. `Identity` with the monoid marker `Additive` names an identity element.

`Lane` is the arithmetic vocabulary a kernel generic over a number is bounded by, with names from arvo's value page: `widen` into an exact accumulator, `wide_add` and `wide_sub` on it, `narrow` back, and `MAX_TERMS`, how many widened members the accumulator holds exactly. `Int<BITS>` is the one proxy lane, signed and saturating, with the accumulator `Wide<BITS>` of `2 * BITS` bits; its term bound is `2^BITS - 1`, since `2^BITS` subtracted minimums overflow the register by one. Frozen authors never name `Int`; tests and the cross-target tool do.

## The digest

`Digest` is the fold a run's state is compared by across runners and targets, and `Absorb` the one way into it. Every word enters as eight little-endian bytes, so nothing that enters depends on the target's pointer width. The fold and the word encoding are unlocked until the journal format locks.

## Principles

`#![no_std]` and no alloc, every crate. Monomorphisation is the dispatch: `Read::get` takes a typed handle, and a runner reaches a kernel through `UnitFn`, never through `dyn` or a type id.
